,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,6785,6291,311,198,59,26056,37018,220,16,45340,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,17,24,92,41715,37018,16,45340,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,19,20,19732,59,37018,220,17,45340,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,21,24,51185,15,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198,1249,11625,279,23606,1124,11520,37018,90,16,15170,87,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,17,24,92,488,1124,37018,90,16,15170,87,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,19,20,92,481,1124,37018,90,17,15170,87,61,17,12,16,15,87,12,21,24,92,284,220,15,59,701,582,1191,553,20194,17767,88,284,856,61,17,481,220,16,15,87,481,220,21,24,59,568,1096,48968,15491,9606,279,23606,438,11017,1447],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the positive solution to\n\\[\\frac 1{x^2-10x-29}+\\frac1{x^2-10x-45}-\\frac 2{x^2-10x-69}=0.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a89c050390bb52450656c670985248046e6fef631b0e91fa2df49efb2e1fb653"},"seconds":35.71941590309143,"seed":1008438626788715234,"stop":"eos","text":"\\[\n\\frac{1}{y + 40} + \\frac{1}{y + 24} - \\frac{2}{y} = 0.\n\\]\n\nNext, we find a common denominator for the fractions, which is \\(y(y + 40)(y + 24)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y(y + 24) + y(y + 40) - 2(y + 40)(y + 24)}{y(y + 40)(y + 24)} = 0.\n\\]\n\nSince the denominator cannot be zero, we focus on the numerator and simplify it:\n\n\\[\ny(y + 24) + y(y + 40) - 2(y + 40)(y + 24) = 0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding each term, we get:\n\n\\[\ny^2 + 24y + y^2 + 40y - 2(y^2 + 64y + 960) = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n2y^2 + 64y - 2y^2 - 128y - 1920 = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n-64y - 1920 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n-64y = 1920,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -30.\n\\]\n\nRecalling our substitution \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 69 = -30,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -10\\), and \\(c = -39\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{26}{2} = 13 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{-6}{2} = -3.\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{1}{x^2-10x-29} + \\frac{1}{x^2-10x-45} - \\frac{2}{x^2-10x-69} = 0\\), we start by letting \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\). This substitution simplifies the equation as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{y + 40} + \\frac{1}{y + 24} - \\frac{2}{y} = 0.\n\\]\n\nNext, we find a common denominator for the fractions, which is \\(y(y + 40)(y + 24)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y(y + 24) + y(y + 40) - 2(y + 40)(y + 24)}{y(y + 40)(y + 24)} = 0.\n\\]\n\nSince the denominator cannot be zero, we focus on the numerator and simplify it:\n\n\\[\ny(y + 24) + y(y + 40) - 2(y + 40)(y + 24) = 0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding each term, we get:\n\n\\[\ny^2 + 24y + y^2 + 40y - 2(y^2 + 64y + 960) = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n2y^2 + 64y - 2y^2 - 128y - 1920 = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n-64y - 1920 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n-64y = 1920,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -30.\n\\]\n\nRecalling our substitution \\(y = x^2 - 10x - 69\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 69 = -30,\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x - 39 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -10\\), and \\(c = -39\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 156}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10 \\pm 16}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{26}{2} = 13 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{-6}{2} = -3.\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:571"}
